<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<chapter xml:id="ch-1-propositional-logic" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Introduction to Propositional Logic</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      What <em>is</em> math?
    </p>

    <p>
      (The question is contrived, but I would encourage you to think about
      it!)
    </p>

    <p>
      To me, most of math can be summarized by following procedure:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>Create some kind of mathematical space.</li>
        <li>Impose additional structure on that space.</li>
        <li>Explore the consequences of that structure and observe
        patterns and other nontrivial results.</li>
        <li>Prove that those results hold.</li>
      </ul>
    </p>

    <p>
      For example, suppose we are interested in a more formal treatment of
      the integers:
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>\mathbb{Z} := \{\ldots, -2, -1, 0, 1, 2, \ldots\}.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      This set is playing the role of the <term>mathematical space</term>
      referenced above. Next, we might be interested in imposing some
      structure on that space. For example, we might define
      <em>addition</em> of integers as a function (<q><m>+</m></q>) that
      takes in two integers (<m>m</m> and <m>n</m>) and spits out a new
      integer (<m>m + n</m>) (in the usual way):
    </p>

    <p>
      <md>+: (m, n) \mapsto m + n.</md>
    </p>

    <p>
      Then, we start to explore the consequences of this structure. For
      example, one might start to notice that every time you add two odd
      numbers together (after you <em>define</em> <q>odd,</q> of course),
      you get an even number:
    </p>

    <p>
    </p>

    <p>
      Is this just a fortunate coincidence of the numbers you chose to
      add, or is there something inherent to the structure of your space
      that gives rise to this result? The only way to know for sure if it
      is the latter is to <em>prove</em> the result using nothing but the
      definition of the structure you've imposed and your understanding of
      mathematical logic. Thus, to really understand math, one must
      understand logic. How else can you be confident that your proofs are
      actually proofs?
    </p>

    <p>
      For the next two chapters, we'll be studying mathematical logic in
      detail to get us ready for proofs in chapter 3.
    </p>
  </introduction>

